In our last lesson, we looked at pauses. In this,

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:33.149
lesson 6, we'll look at avoiding fillers.
Concise tip number six is to avoid

00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:37.770
fillers. I call it a concise tip because
certainly avoiding fillers is going to

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:42.239
make you more concise. It will also add a
lot to your clarity and to your

00:00:42.239 --> 00:00:47.910
confident sound. Fillers drag out and
muddle messages for listeners and fillers

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:52.530
make you sound very unsure of what
you're saying. Here are some common fillers.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:01:00.480
Right, sort of, kind of, something like
that. As well as um, uh, you know, so, and.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.680
I know some people that count ums and uhs during presentations. But it's not just

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.979
annoying. It also really goes to your
credibility.

00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:12.930
There's some research on powerless vs
powerful speech and when listeners in

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:18.060
research situations were shown videos
of speakers who use lots of fillers they

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.920
thought they were very unconfident or
powerless in their speech. They'd give the

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:28.710
same speakers a clean script so they would
not use any fillers and the listeners in

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.500
the research situation thought wow they
really know what they're talking about.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:36.750
They're very confident. So you can sound
confident just by getting rid of your

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.619
fillers. It's a really important issue to
get after. Strategy one is to identify

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:44.999
your filler habits. These are habits.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:50.939
Most people have specific fillers that
they use over and over again. I use "kind

00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:55.380
of," I used to use that. I also use "so" and
I'm working on that one but I still use

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:00.980
it from time to time. You want to listen
to yourself and pinpoint your specific habits.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:04.619
Listen to recorded presentations, for
example, or you might even ask a trusted

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:09.929
friend. Hey, do I use any fillers do I say
you know or anything like that? And even

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:13.129
if they don't have an answer for you on
the spot, they'll be able to listen to you

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:19.070
as you move forward. Strategy two is to
replace fillers with pauses. I talked about

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:23.600
this in an earlier lesson how awesome
pauses can sound in the right place.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.230
Some people call fillers "vocalized
pauses." In other words, we should be

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:31.670
pausing but we're filling it in with an
utterance that doesn't need to be there.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:38.150
Pauses give you time to think but they
also make you sound more confident.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:43.280
Here we have an earlier example with fillers put in there where pauses should be and

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:48.320
you'll notice how tentative and how
unsure it sounds. So, we have a few items

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:53.360
to cover today and uh and we'll look at our
social media campaign and uh we'll look at uh

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.390
our overall growth and uh Erin will take
you know suggestions about changes to

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:04.250
the uh website. If I were to come to your
meeting and speak like this you would

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:09.500
notice instantly that I had lots of
fillers. And I have seen people who are

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.460
supposed to be in leadership positions
doing this and listeners, in fact, did

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:18.470
mention, wow, they said um and uh a lot.
This matters to people. What we want to

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:24.170
do is add pauses in those places where
we normally use fillers. And we would say the

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:29.450
word "pause" or "period" in our head like we mentioned in a previous lesson. Pauses

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:34.580
restore that clear, confident sound that
we're going for. And you'll notice that

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:39.860
there are some pauses in the middle here
but they still sound better than the fillers.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:43.670
So if we were to look at this example
ultimately our goal would be to get rid

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:47.780
of the fillers that are in the middle of
the sentences and only use the pauses

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:54.740
that punctuate our communication in a
desired fashion. We want to avoid fillers.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:04:01.720
Our takeaway strategies are to identify
your specific filler habits. You don't do them all.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:06.140
You just do some and if you isolate your
specific habits you're going to have a much

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:10.490
easier time solving that problem. And
then replace those fillers with actual

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:18.080
pauses. Step one is to practice saying "pause" in your head instead of using fillers.

